From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kurt Fuchs <contact@fuchskurt.eu>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 13:03:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] build: add a firmware profile with immediate-abort and
 stripped symbols

`cargo build --profile firmware` inherits the release profile (opt-level z,
fat LTO, one codegen unit) and adds panic = "immediate-abort" and
strip = "symbols". The x86_64 locked payload shrinks from 2,711,160 to
2,396,952 bytes. Panics still halt: the trap reaches the IDT exception
handler, which logs the RIP and halts, but the panic message is not printed.

The profile is separate because the workspace's host tools (for example
crabefi-varstore-gen) link the prebuilt std, which rejects immediate-abort.
---
 Cargo.toml | 14 ++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Cargo.toml b/Cargo.toml
index a1d2957..b0826bf 100644
--- a/Cargo.toml
+++ b/Cargo.toml
@@ -1,3 +1,6 @@
+# `panic = "immediate-abort"` (profile.firmware) is still unstable in Cargo.
+cargo-features = ["panic-immediate-abort"]
+
 # Workspace: the core library and platform payloads are separate crates.
 # xtask and test-apps are NOT workspace members (different build targets).
 
@@ -51,3 +54,14 @@ panic = "abort"
 lto = true
 opt-level = "z"
 codegen-units = 1
+
+# Firmware payload only: `cargo build --profile firmware`. The host tools in
+# this workspace link Rust's prebuilt std, which cannot use immediate-abort,
+# so this cannot live in profile.release. A panic becomes a trap instruction:
+# the IDT handler logs "Invalid Opcode" and the RIP, then halts (no message).
+[profile.firmware]
+inherits = "release"
+# Same as release's `lto = true` (rustc -C lto); spelled out because it matters.
+lto = "fat"
+panic = "immediate-abort"
+strip = "symbols"
